\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}

\title{Headway Adherence on the Route 7 Corridor:\newline A Year of Automatic Vehicle Location Records}
\author{D.~Ferreira and C.~Nakamura}
\date{April 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Automatic vehicle location records for the Route 7 corridor were analyzed for the full 2025 service year.
Headway adherence improved after the July schedule revision, most strongly at the evening peak.
Bunching remained concentrated at the two timed transfer points.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Route 7 is the busiest surface line in the municipal network.
The corridor runs from Harbor View to Old Mill with nine scheduled stops.
This report reviews a year of location records and summarizes where headways held and where they broke.

\section{Data}
The analysis covers the nine scheduled stops on the corridor: Pine Street, Depot Square, Riverside, Mill Bridge, Northgate, Harbor View, Elm Crossing, South Ferry, and Old Mill.
Records came from the fleet telematics feed at thirty-second resolution.
Days with fewer than forty trips were dropped from the analysis.

\section{Method}
Headways were computed at each stop from successive arrivals.
Trips were grouped into weekday, Saturday, and Sunday patterns.
Bunching was flagged whenever two vehicles arrived within ninety seconds.

\section{Results}
Median headway deviation fell from 4.1 to 2.8 minutes after the July schedule revision.
Evening peaks improved the most, while Sunday service stayed flat.
Bunching events clustered at Northgate and South Ferry, the two timed transfer points.

